// Demonstration of the `flowchart` package — program flowcharts as a thin layer of
// conventions over the `diagram` package. The flowchart package names the diagram shapes
// by their roles (terminal, process, decision, input/output, subroutine) and reads the
// edges as flow. This script exercises every node role, every edge router and branch
// label, the configuration knobs, and a complete realistic algorithm flowchart.
//
// Render with:  texish scripts/flowchart-demo.script

\use{document}
\use{flowchart}

\def headline {\italic{The flowchart package} \hfil \the\pageno}
\def footline {\hfil — \the\pageno — \hfil}
\def centerline c {\hbox to:\hsize {\hfil\c\hfil}}

\title{The \texttt{flowchart} Package}
\author{A texish demonstration}
\maketitle

\section{The five node roles}

A flowchart is built inside a \verb|\picture| from \italic{nodes} and \italic{edges}.
Each node is declared by its role; the package maps that role to the standard
ISO-1028 / ANSI shape programmers expect. There are five roles. A \italic{terminal} —
declared with \verb|\start| or \verb|\stop| — is a rounded ``stadium'' marking the entry
and exit points. A \italic{process} is a plain rectangle: an action or computation. A
\italic{decision} is a diamond, a branch whose outgoing edges are labelled. An
\italic{io} node is a parallelogram for input or output. A \italic{subroutine} is a
rectangle with side bars, denoting a call to a predefined process.

Every role takes the same three arguments as the underlying \verb|\node|: a name, a
placement spec, and a label. The figure below shows all five shapes side by side.

\vskip 8pt

{\set dgfont {9}
\centerline{\picture width:5.4in height:1.2in {
  \start{t}{at 0.5in 0.6in}{Start}
  \process{p}{right t 14}{Process}
  \decision{d}{right p 14}{Decision?}
  \io{i}{right d 18}{Read / Print}
  \subroutine{s}{right i 14}{Subroutine}
}}}

\section{Placement specifications}

A node is placed either at an absolute coordinate or relative to a node already declared.
The placement spec is a small word language: \verb|at X Y| gives an absolute coordinate,
while \verb|below ref|, \verb|above ref|, \verb|right ref| and \verb|left ref| position a
node against another's boundary. Any relative form accepts an optional trailing gap in
points — \verb|below test 46| widens the default spacing — so neighbours sit a true gap
apart whatever their sizes. Relative placement is what lets a flowchart be written top to
bottom in reading order.

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:4.6in height:1.7in {
  \process{c}{at 2.3in 0.85in}{centre}
  \process{ab}{above c 18}{above}
  \process{be}{below c 18}{below}
  \process{le}{left c 24}{left}
  \process{ri}{right c 24}{right}
}}

\section{Edges and branch labels}

Edges read as flow. A \verb|\flow| is a straight arrow along the path of control; a
\verb|\branch| leaves horizontally and then turns down into its target (the natural route
for a decision's side exit); and a \verb|\rejoin| drops vertically and then runs back
across to an earlier box (a feedback or merge line). Each takes an optional label in
brackets, passed straight through to the arrow — and an omitted label simply draws no
text. A decision's two exits are conventionally labelled \italic{yes} and \italic{no}.

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:4.4in height:2.6in {
  \decision{q}{at 1.1in 2.15in}{x > 0?}
  \process{neg}{right q 70}{handle −x}
  \process{pos}{below q 30}{handle x}
  \stop{end}{below pos 36}{done}

  \flow[yes]{q}{pos}
  \branch[no]{q}{neg}
  \flow{pos}{end}
  \rejoin{neg}{end}
}}

The straight \verb|\flow| carries the \italic{yes} branch down into the process; the
\verb|\branch| sends \italic{no} across and down to the right-hand process; and the
\verb|\rejoin| from that process drops and merges back into the terminal.

\section{A complete algorithm: summing 1 to n}

Putting it together, here is a genuine algorithm flowchart with a counting loop and a
decision. The sum $\sum_{i=1}^{n} i$ is computed iteratively: read $n$, initialise the
accumulator, then test $i \le n$. The decision branches \italic{yes} down into the loop
body, which curves back up to test again, and \italic{no} across to print the result.

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:4.6in height:5in {
  \start{begin}{at 1.5in 4.6in}{Start}
  \io{read}{below begin}{Read $n$}
  \process{init}{below read}{$s \gets 0,\quad i \gets 1$}
  \decision{test}{below init}{$i \le n$?}
  \process{body}{below test 46}{$s \gets s + i,\quad i \gets i + 1$}
  \io{out}{right test 96}{Print $s$}
  \stop{done}{below out}{Stop}

  \flow{begin}{read}
  \flow{read}{init}
  \flow{init}{test}
  \flow[yes]{test}{body}
  \cedge[58]{body}{test}{}
  \flow[no]{test}{out}
  \flow{out}{done}
}}

The feedback arrow from the loop body back to the decision is drawn with the underlying
diagram primitive \verb|\cedge|, a curved edge bowed sideways. The flowchart package's
own \verb|\flow|, \verb|\branch| and \verb|\rejoin| cover the common routes, but every
diagram edge and every configuration knob remains available for anything the conventions
do not.

\section{A subroutine call with validation}

The \verb|\subroutine| role marks a call to a predefined process. Here a request is read,
validated by a decision, and — when valid — handed to a subroutine that processes it
before the result is printed. An invalid request rejoins the exit directly.

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:4.8in height:4.4in {
  \start{s0}{at 1.5in 4.05in}{Begin}
  \io{s1}{below s0}{Read request}
  \decision{s2}{below s1}{Valid?}
  \subroutine{s3}{below s2 40}{Process(request)}
  \io{s4}{below s3}{Print result}
  \stop{s5}{below s4}{End}
  \process{s6}{right s2 90}{Log error}

  \flow{s0}{s1}
  \flow{s1}{s2}
  \flow[yes]{s2}{s3}
  \flow{s3}{s4}
  \flow{s4}{s5}
  \branch[no]{s2}{s6}
  \rejoin{s6}{s5}
}}

\section{Configuration}

Because the flowchart package is a preset over \verb|diagram|, all of diagram's
configuration variables apply. They are set with
\verb|\set name {value}| after the \verb|\use|, and respect grouping — set inside a brace
group they affect only the figures within it. Below, the same little flowchart is drawn
twice: once with the defaults, then with a warmer fill, tighter gap and smaller label
font.

\vskip 8pt

\centerline{\picture width:2.6in height:2.2in {
  \start{a0}{at 1.3in 1.85in}{Start}
  \process{a1}{below a0}{step}
  \stop{a2}{below a1}{Stop}
  \flow{a0}{a1}
  \flow{a1}{a2}
}}

\vskip 6pt

{\set dgfill {#fdebd0}
 \set dggap {16}
 \set dgfont {9}
 \set dgedgewidth {0.9pt}
\centerline{\picture width:2.6in height:1.8in {
  \start{b0}{at 1.3in 1.5in}{Start}
  \process{b1}{below b0}{step}
  \stop{b2}{below b1}{Stop}
  \flow{b0}{b1}
  \flow{b1}{b2}
}}}

Every figure in this document is a single \verb|\picture| box flowing in the text,
typeset by the same engine as the prose around it.
